well joining us now is dr michael greg  citis a consultant pediatric intensive  care doctor in southampton who was part  of the team that helped bring the  children over hello to you doctor thank  you for joining us um these little ones  have cancer but they are now uh here  safe in the uk tell us about their  journey  yeah thank you i would like to start off  by saying that actually  this was an incredible collaboration  across all parts of the nhs the  department of health and primarily  frontline children's hospitals that were  able to put together  um  critical care transport teams supported  by oncology colleagues  made up from university hospital of  southampton and birmingham children's  hospital and so we flew out to retrieve  21 children  receiving cancer therapies and with  actually very little information and  arrived to find  a situation that we were  you know we had no expectations of and  flew them back yesterday back to the uk  um successfully and this all happened  quite quickly quite efficiently and was  quite an emotional experience actually  obviously we want to not probe too  deeply into these um youngsters  conditions but i wondered if you could  give us an idea of their ages and and  what sort of conditions we're talking  about  yes actually i obviously can't go into  individual cases but i can tell you that  uh generically the age range was was  vast going from uh babies through to  teenagers um and  there was all different kinds of cancers  on the flight and it wasn't just one  type there was many many types of  different cancers  and  these children were all  of  varying degrees of illness some  critically ill and and  we were  just privileged and honored to be able  to help uh get them out of the country  and and get them to  england and the uk where we could  provide  support for them because without this  ongoing care they would die  how  did you choose which children needed the  most help  um we we didn't so there was triage  systems and in poland and  various children were allocated to  various different agencies and we made  it clear from the pediatric intensive  care transport team from southampton  that we would be able to move children  of any level of critical illness  and would not turn children away if  people were worried about how sick they  were and we were able to move all 21  that we were asked  what was their journey like  emotional um they started off obviously  afraid and these  some of the stories of what these  children have been through you i i can't  even begin to imagine  um  and  to have that on its own is bad enough  for anyone but to have that whilst  having treatment for cancer it was just  astronomical so when they got on that  plane and they were scared they were  afraid and language barrier was a  problem we had some incredible  translators and some incredible player  therapists for the children that were  well enough to to be able to um  communicate and talk and interact  and actually as the plane took off you  almost saw a sense of relief that these  families knew that their children were  going to get treatment  and that they had a chance of  surviving their cancer  and actually on landing children that  initially wouldn't make eye contact and  were terrified were high-fiving playing  smiling and it was um just a really it  was it was an honor to be able to  offer the southampton services for the  critical care part of that transport  team and to work with the birmingham  children's hospital oncology team to  deliver this  and before i let you go doctor um i get  the impression that there will be  uh more children who will be able to  make the journey as well what can you  tell us about that  all i can say is that um when this  mission was requested and it was  a fairly straightforward decision to say  yes we will happily offer help  and i have no doubt that the same answer  will occur if we get asked again
 

